<mujoco model="two_finger_hand">
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="meshes" autolimits="true"/>
  <!--
    Collision uses the real meshes, matching how the stock UMI gripper is set up
    (assets/umi_gripper: `<default class="collision"><geom type="mesh" group="3"/>`).
    MuJoCo collides meshes as their convex hull, and these Onshape exports hull to
    ~2400 vertices per finger -- 58x the UMI finger's 42 -- which is what
    overflowed mujoco-warp's fixed EPA polytope and produced NaNs. `maxhullvert`
    caps the collision hull at UMI-like complexity while keeping the true finger
    shape: the flat 27x21 mm inner pad and its 4.3 deg slope, both of which the
    previous capsule/box primitives got wrong (round surface -> line contact, and
    the jaw read 22 mm narrower than the real fingers when open).

    Mass convention also follows UMI: the visual geoms are density="0" and the
    collision geoms carry the inertia, so mass is not double-counted (the old
    XML had both the visual meshes and the primitives contributing).
  -->
  <asset>
    <mesh name="base_link" file="base_link.stl" maxhullvert="64"/>
    <mesh name="adapter" file="adapter.stl"/>
    <mesh name="shim" file="shim.stl"/>
    <mesh name="mount24" file="mount24.stl"/>
    <mesh name="left_1" file="left_1.stl" maxhullvert="48"/>
    <mesh name="left_2" file="left_2.stl" maxhullvert="48"/>
    <mesh name="right_1" file="right_1.stl" maxhullvert="48"/>
    <mesh name="right_2" file="right_2.stl" maxhullvert="48"/>
  </asset>
  <default>
    <default class="visual">
      <geom type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="1" density="0"/>
    </default>
    <default class="collision">
      <geom type="mesh" group="3" rgba="0.9 0.25 0.15 0.6"/>
    </default>
  </default>
  <worldbody>
    <body name="base_link">
      <geom mesh="base_link" rgba="0.80 0.82 0.85 1" class="visual"/>
      <geom mesh="adapter" rgba="0.60 0.75 0.60 1" class="visual" pos="0.0 0.0113 0.0315" quat="0 1 0 0"/>
      <geom mesh="shim" rgba="0.72 0.55 0.86 1" class="visual" pos="0.0 0.0113 0.0075" quat="0 0 1 0"/>
      <geom mesh="mount24" rgba="0.90 0.59 0.24 1" class="visual" pos="-0.035 0.0108 0.0005" quat="1 0 0 0"/>
      <geom name="palm_col" mesh="base_link" class="collision"/>
      <body name="left_1" pos="-0.028 -0.0037 -0.024">
        <joint name="left_1" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.6 1.6" armature="0.01" damping="0.2" solreflimit="0.02 1"/>
        <geom mesh="left_1" rgba="0.42 0.62 0.90 1" class="visual"/>
        <geom name="left_1_col" mesh="left_1" class="collision"/>
        <body name="left_2" pos="0 0 -0.0445">
          <joint name="left_2" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.6 1.6" armature="0.01" damping="0.2" solreflimit="0.02 1"/>
          <geom mesh="left_2" rgba="0.10 0.30 0.70 1" class="visual"/>
          <geom name="left_2_col" mesh="left_2" class="collision"/>
          <!--
            A FINGERTIP reference point, chosen deliberately -- read this before
            treating it as a contact point, because for the 50 mm cube it is not
            one.

            History, all measured (scripts/diag_pad_site.py, diag_pad_contact.py,
            diag_finger_profile.py, diag_pad_candidates.py):
              (9.54, 8.85, -24.42)  centre of the flat inner face. The XML called
                                    this the gripping face and it is, but the cube
                                    loads the face's BOTTOM EDGE, 7.1 mm lower.
              (9.97, 8.85, -31.49)  that bottom edge -- where a 50 mm cube really
                                    bears, stable to 0.01 mm across distal curls
                                    -0.20..-0.60, and coincident with the contact
                                    point to 0.01 mm.
              (1.33, 18.85, -44.00) HERE. Further toward the tip by choice.

            What changes at -44 mm: the flat pad ENDS at z = -31.5. Below it the
            finger tapers hard -- inner surface x is +10.0 at -32, +6.9 at -36,
            +4.2 at -40, +1.3 at -44 -- so x follows the surface rather than
            staying at the pad's +10.0, or the site would hang in free space in
            front of the finger. y moves too: the surface at this height spans
            +10.8..+25.9, and the old +8.85 is outside it. +18.85 is half way
            from the old y to the far edge.

            CONSEQUENCE, do not lose this. What matters is not the drop in z but
            the drop in x: the flat gripping face is at x = +10.0, and x = +1.33
            is 8.6 mm BEHIND it, toward the finger's spine. Both fingers pull
            back by that much, so the pair sits 17.2 mm wider apart than the two
            gripping faces do. Measured while actually gripping a 50 mm cube
            (fingers stopped by the cube at left_1 = +0.234):

              old site (9.97, ., -31.49)  separation 49.9 mm -- ON the cube face
              this site (1.33, ., -44.00) separation 64.4 mm -- 7.2 mm CLEAR of
                                          the cube on each side, in mid-air

            So this point can never coincide with a contact. `pad_pinch` pulls
            both sites at the cube centre and the nearest they can get is 7.2 mm
            away, with the fingers already hard against the cube -- asking for
            more only shoves the cube. Run 45608 scored grasp 0.339 against
            0.468 for the same config on the old site.

            (An earlier version of this note claimed the two tips were 2.7 mm
            apart and that the cube was therefore out of reach INSIDE them. Both
            were wrong: 2.7 mm was the difference of two link-frame x
            coordinates, which is not a distance between the fingers at all, and
            the sites sit outside the cube, not inside it.)

            Anything comparing this separation against an object width must use
            a measured value, not 2*CUBE_HALF; see antipodal_pinch in
            flexiv_two_finger/env_cfgs.py.
          -->
          <site name="left_pad" pos="0.00133 0.01885 -0.04400" size="0.004"
                rgba="0.2 0.5 1.0 0.9"/>
        </body>
      </body>
      <body name="right_1" pos="0.028 -0.0037 -0.024">
        <joint name="right_1" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.6 1.6" armature="0.01" damping="0.2" solreflimit="0.02 1"/>
        <geom mesh="right_1" rgba="0.92 0.48 0.48 1" class="visual"/>
        <geom name="right_1_col" mesh="right_1" class="collision"/>
        <body name="right_2" pos="0 0 -0.0445">
          <joint name="right_2" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.6 1.6" armature="0.01" damping="0.2" solreflimit="0.02 1"/>
          <geom mesh="right_2" rgba="0.62 0.0 0.0 1" class="visual"/>
          <geom name="right_2_col" mesh="right_2" class="collision"/>
          <!-- Mirror of left_pad; see the note there. -->
          <site name="right_pad" pos="-0.00133 0.01885 -0.04400" size="0.004"
                rgba="1.0 0.3 0.3 0.9"/>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
  <actuator>
    <position name="left_1" joint="left_1" kp="1"/>
    <position name="left_2" joint="left_2" kp="1"/>
    <position name="right_1" joint="right_1" kp="1"/>
    <position name="right_2" joint="right_2" kp="1"/>
  </actuator>
</mujoco>
